CORE
main.c
--unwind 6 --infer-rounds
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^Round bound (static): count=11 path=6 
^Round bound (property cone): count=6 path=4 blocks=
^Infer rounds: static round bound 4$
^Infer rounds: violation found with 3 round(s)
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
The value 8 reaches the assertion only along t1 <-> t2 -> t3 -> main: j is 8
after four alternations of t1 and t2 (3 rounds, --rounds 2 is SUCCESSFUL),
t3 copies it to x and main reads x. t4 and t5 ping-pong on a and b, which
nothing in that chain reads, and push the bound on every block to 6. The cone
of the assertion follows main's read of x to t3's write, t3's read of j to the
writes of t2, and from there to t1: it keeps the chain and drops t4 and t5, so
the bound is 4 >= 3 and --infer-rounds finds the bug. Following only program
order (no flow edges) the cone would be main alone, the bound 1, and the
answer a wrong SUCCESSFUL.
